  "id": "COMPOUND:19392 - PROTEIN:5867",
  "verbalization": "Islandicin 4-Methyl Ether is shown to have a target relationship with HaCaT through multiple pathways, all of which converge on the cell surface receptor MIP as a key intermediate. The first pathway links Islandicin 4-Methyl Ether to Macrophage scavenger receptor types I and II, which are then targeted by 7-Hydroxy-6-(2-(R)-Hydroxy-3-Methylbut-3-Enyl)Coumarin, a compound that in turn targets HaCaT. The second pathway directly connects Islandicin 4-Methyl Ether to MIP, which is subsequently targeted by a complex compound with a specific structure, also leading to HaCaT. The third pathway similarly links Islandicin 4-Methyl Ether to MIP, which is targeted by a more complex compound. These findings suggest that Islandicin 4-Methyl Ether may act through a mechanism involving MIP, potentially modulating cellular processes in HaCaT cells. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between the drug and HaCaT, though the exact mode of action remains to be fully elucidated. The presence of multiple pathways involving MIP and different downstream targets indicates a possible role in cell surface signaling or immune response modulation.",
